#!/bin/sh
# X server command line for startx, installed as /etc/X11/xinit/xserverrc.
#
# startx runs THIS instead of the server directly whenever it was given no
# server arguments of its own.  It still appends the display and the -auth file
# holding the MIT-MAGIC-COOKIE-1 record it generated, so those arrive here as
# "$@" and have to be passed through -- dropping them would start a server with
# no access control at all.
#
# A normal user can run startx, and needs no group membership to do it:
# /usr/bin/Xorg is installed setuid root (see ports/xorg/stage.sh).
#
# Why setuid rather than groups.  The server has to open the framebuffer and
# the event devices, which are root-owned, and there is no seat manager here to
# hand those to whoever is logged in at the console.  The obvious alternative
# -- putting the user in the `video` and `input` groups -- grants that account
# permanent read/write access to the keyboard and the screen: enough to read
# every keystroke typed into any session on the machine, root's included, and
# to read back what any session is displaying, for as long as the account
# exists and whether or not X is running.  A setuid binary that gives root up
# the moment its devices are open is the smaller grant, and the server does
# exactly that: it drops real, effective and saved IDs together once
# InitOutput() and InitAndStartDevices() have finished, before it serves a
# single client, and verifies the drop took effect
# (ports/xorg/patches/xorg-server/0005-drop-privileges-after-device-init.patch).
#
# The log therefore stays at the server's default, /var/log/Xorg.0.log: it is
# opened -- and rotated -- while the server is still root, then written through
# the descriptor it already holds.  No per-user log directory is needed, and no
# -logfile is passed: a setuid server refuses an unsafe path for it anyway.

# -noreset: do nothing when the last client disconnects.
#
# Not a preference.  The default is to RESET -- tear the server down to its
# initial state and re-run the initialisation, which is what opens the input
# devices.  A server that has dropped root cannot reopen them, so a reset would
# bring it back up with no keyboard and no pointer.
#
# -terminate would avoid that too, but it is a trap here: xinit waits for the
# server by connecting and then closing that connection, and closing the LAST
# connection is exactly what arms the terminate.  The session would be racing
# its own display server's exit.  Doing nothing is what is wanted, and xinit
# stops the server itself when the session ends.
#
# -nolisten tcp: this server is built with LISTEN_TCP=no, so the option only
# states the intent explicitly and keeps it true if that build setting ever
# changes.  Clients connect over the AF_UNIX socket and are authorised by the
# cookie in $XAUTHORITY, which is what stops another local user from attaching
# to this session's display.
# Which configuration to run with.
#
# Two of them: /etc/X11/xorg.conf drives /dev/fb0 through the fbdev driver and
# renders in software, and works on any machine that boots to a framebuffer;
# /etc/X11/xorg.conf.modesetting drives the display manager through
# /dev/dri/card0 with glamor and DRI3, which needs a GPU the kernel has a
# driver for.  The node is the test: it exists only where such a driver bound
# to a device, so probing for it here picks the accelerated configuration
# exactly where it can work and leaves the fallback in place everywhere else
# -- no per-machine editing, and one image for both.
#
# Named relative, not as a path.  Xorg is setuid root (see below), and a
# server with elevated privileges rejects an absolute -config argument
# outright; a bare name is looked up in /etc/X11, which is where the file is.
if [ -e /dev/dri/card0 ]; then
	set -- -config xorg.conf.modesetting "$@"
fi

exec /usr/bin/Xorg -noreset -nolisten tcp "$@"
